WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.501
Imagine a new lemonade stall at your school fair. A big drinks company sets up next to it and sells drinks below what they cost to make, just to drive the stall away.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:33.296
Countries face threats like this from imports. Protection means government measures that restrict imports, to shield domestic producers or consumers. A tariff, a tax on imports, shifts import supply left: price rises and fewer imports are bought.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:47.600
Dumping is a firm or country selling exports abroad at a price below its cost of production. Firms dump to get rid of surplus output, or to drive foreign rivals out and win market share.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:01:01.420
Infant industries are new industries that have not yet grown big enough to compete. Small firms have high average costs. Protection gives them time to grow and gain economies of scale.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:12.302
Other reasons are to protect domestic jobs, keep out unsafe goods, raise tax revenue from tariffs, reduce a current account deficit, and retaliate against another country's barriers.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:27.498
Step one, name the motive. Step two, explain the threat the imports pose. Step three, show what happens if nothing is done, or how restricting imports removes the threat.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:34.707
Step four, use the case: the product, the country, the event. Step five, for a definition, give both parts.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:59.822
Here is a real question from November 2020. In December 2018, UK consumers were warned to avoid some imported plastic toys containing banned chemicals. Many were sold online. Explain one reason why UK consumers need protecting from some imported goods. Three marks.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:13.327
The reason is safety. Some imports are made in countries with weaker safety rules, so they may not meet UK standards. Here, toys contained banned chemicals and were sold online.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:32.389
Develop it. Parents cannot tell the toys are unsafe. Children play with them and could become seriously ill. So the government restricts these imports to protect UK consumers. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:42.899
A common mistake is defining dumping as just selling cheaply. It must be abroad, and below the cost of production.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:50.050
Another is describing how a tariff works when the question asks why a country wants protection.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:03:11.196
Now you try. A country has just started making solar panels. Its firms are small, and cheaper panels arrive from large foreign firms. Explain one reason why the government may protect its solar panel industry. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:22.199
This is an infant industry. The firms are new and small, so they cannot yet match the low prices of large foreign rivals.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:33.594
Protection gives them time to grow. As output rises, they gain economies of scale and average cost falls, until they can compete without help.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:43.094
Remember: name the motive, explain the threat from imports, show the consequence, and use the case. Now try the practice questions.
